Russian speakers have problems with:
	short vowels and long vowels, in Russian there is no difference
	ɪ and i:, in Russian there is no ɪ
	ʌ and ɑ:
	ʌ and ə
In American English ɑ: and ɒ are one vowel, so calm and cot have the same vowel, hot is written as hɑ:t
40% of Americans pronounce ɔ: the same way as ɑ: (so that caught and cot have the same vowel)
Americans pronounce t as true t, flap t (which sounds like d) and stop t, also:
	tr may sound as ch
	t may be dropped after n (center, internet, international) and between two other consonants (exactly, perfectly)
		and when two words link (just because)
Between vowels and before and after voiced consonant s is usually pronounced as z (housing, husband, hunters)
After unvoiced consonant -ed is usually pronounced as t (talked, parked)

